From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] fcode: consume "instance" in b(value), b(variable), b(defer)
 and b(buffer:)

"instance" sets a flag that the next defining word consumes through
instance?. The Forth defining words do, the FCode ones don't, so after an
FCode program has used "instance" the flag stays set until some later
Forth defining word picks it up. The Solaris 10 boot block is such a
program: the kernel's "defer p@" in data64.fth then became an instance
defer, and instance, read the instance size through active-package,
which is 0 there:

  Unhandled Exception 0x00000000ffeb6520
  PC = 0x00000000ffd0f220 NPC = 0x00000000ffd0f224    (in "@")

Clear the flag in the four FCode defining words. They still create
static data, as before.
---
 forth/device/fcode.fs | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/forth/device/fcode.fs b/forth/device/fcode.fs
index 9083ed0..42569f2 100644
--- a/forth/device/fcode.fs
+++ b/forth/device/fcode.fs
@@ -269,6 +269,7 @@ defer fcode-c@             \ get byte
 \   Defines type of new FCode function as buffer:.
 
 : b(buffer:)
+  instance? drop      \ consume "instance" (FCode instance data isn't supported)
   4 , allot
   reveal
   ;
@@ -296,6 +297,7 @@ defer fcode-c@             \ get byte
 \   Defines type of new FCode function as defer word.
 
 : b(defer)
+  instance? drop      \ consume "instance" (FCode instance data isn't supported)
   5 ,
   ['] (undefined-defer) ,
   ['] (semis) ,
@@ -321,6 +323,7 @@ defer fcode-c@             \ get byte
 \   Defines type of new FCode function as value.
   
 : b(value)
+  instance? drop      \ consume "instance" (FCode instance data isn't supported)
   3 , , reveal
   ;
 
@@ -329,6 +332,7 @@ defer fcode-c@             \ get byte
 \   Defines type of new FCode function as variable.
 
 : b(variable)
+  instance? drop      \ consume "instance" (FCode instance data isn't supported)
   4 , 0 ,
   reveal
   ;
